# 5. Hardware Manager

The Hardware Manager became the major architectural stabilization point of the build.

Its purpose is to perform hardware discovery once, centrally and consistently.

The manager:

1. Scans candidate serial devices.
2. Identifies the USB/CH340 converters.
3. Opens candidate serial ports.
4. Resets the MCU through DTR.
5. Waits for the MCU to announce its EEPROM identity.
6. Parses EID, TYPE, SENSOR, and SENSOR2.
7. Associates the identity with the current `/dev/ttyUSB#`.
8. Builds a device registry.
9. Publishes the registry on `/hardware/device_map`.

The map is published using reliable, transient-local ROS QoS so that newly launched entry nodes can receive the already-established device map without requiring another hardware discovery cycle.

The manager performs the physical discovery/reset operation **once at startup**.

It then republishes its known map periodically without repeatedly resetting the devices.

This was an important simplification because it prevents each individual sensor node from repeatedly touching the USB hardware.

