# 10. Ultrasonic Integration

The ultrasonic system was integrated as a distance entry node under Hardware Manager control.

The MCU emitted raw readings such as:

```text
ultrasonic:103.9
ultrasonic:89.2
ultrasonic:80.5
ultrasonic:41.0
```

The ROS entry node converted the measurements to millimeters and published:

```text
/ultrasonic_distance
```

The node rejects invalid values, including zero and readings outside the configured useful range.

A behavior observed during testing was that invalid measurements stop producing new messages while the last valid value remains available.

This was treated as acceptable for the current prototype rather than redesigning the sensor/state architecture during the integration push.

---

# 11. ToF Integration

The ToF sensor presented several challenges during the build.

The device correctly announced its identity:

```text
EID:DWUSA-01-03-01,TYPE:DISTANCE,SENSOR:TOF
```

but the VL53L0X hardware initially reported:

```text
tof:error - Failed to boot VL53L0X
```

This appeared to be at least partly related to the physical/mechanical arrangement of the device.

After the physical mounting was changed, valid ToF distance readings appeared.

The final ROS interface is:

```text
/tof_distance
```

using:

```text
std_msgs/msg/Float32
```

The ToF path is now functioning as part of the integrated sensor stack.

An additional software problem was discovered when the CYD debug node had originally subscribed to `/tof_distance` using `std_msgs/msg/Int32` while the ToF node published `Float32`.

That produced a ROS topic type conflict.

The CYD subscription was subsequently changed to `Float32`.

This was a useful example of a problem that was not caused by the physical sensor at all, but by a message-type mismatch at the ROS integration boundary.

